body:
I believe this is trying to say that if you do

```js
history.back();
location.hash = " foo";
```

then the latter wins and the former gets ignoed. I'm not sure if that's true in practice even, but I think that's the idea.

> Would it work to flatten things out as follows? Probably not, but I'd be interested to know why:
> 
> ```
> Traversable
> ┣━ Session history traversal queue. A parallel queue, used to edit…
> ┗┓ Session history entries. A list, each has:
>  ┣━ Document state
>  ┣━ ID (referencing a navigable's ID) 
>  ┗━ Session history entries. A list.
> ```

It would be possible to store it like that, but I think it would be error-prone.

There are some elements of a history entry that are unique to that entry, such its step, the URL, and `history.state`. I put these [directly on the history entry](https://whatpr.org/html/6315/history.html#session-history-entry).

However, there are other bits of state that are more to do with the _document_, which can be shared between multiple history entries (via hash changes and `pushState`). This includes stuff like:

- The document itself
- The browsing context
- `srcdoc` data, or POST data
- And the session history of nested navigables

To express that these can be shared across multiple history entries I nested them in a [document state](https://whatpr.org/html/6315/history.html#she-document-state). This means multiple history entries can point to the same document state. If the document is discarded, or needs to be recreated, it only needs to be done in one place.

The current spec does go for a flatter model, but it's a source of bugs. It uses language like "update all the entries where document is _document_" in things like reloads/repopulations, but that doesn't make sense if the document has been discarded. It also just plain doesn't handle things like `srcdoc` and POST data.

> Also, trying to figure out where the four session history entries from your diagram are located, I still get confused, mainly because I think of navigables as owning their own list of session history entries, so instead saying I have to traverse from traversible -> pick a session history entry -> document state -> nested histories -> pick a nested history -> session history entries -> find the nested session history entry is a lot.

Fwiw, this is why the "get session history entries" algorithm exists. It hides most of that complexity.

> Is it something like this? (example)

It doesn't look like your example is sharing document state between the two entries. For this timeline:

<img alt="Screenshot 2021-06-11 at 07 11 39" width="620" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/93594/121639355-54ac3d80-ca84-11eb-9bb6-bcbb3c801601.png">

The data structure would be like:

```js
const documentState1 = {
  nestedHistories: [
    {
      id: "iframe-1-id",
      entries: [
        {
          step: 1,
          url: "//yo",
          documentState: {
            // …
          },
        },
        {
          step: 3,
          url: "//hello",
          documentState: {
            // …
          },
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
};

const traversable = {
  sessionHistoryEntries: [
    {
      step: 1,
      url: "//app/",
      documentState: documentState1,
    },
    {
      step: 2,
      url: "//app/#foo",
      documentState: documentState1,
    },
    {
      step: 4,
      url: "//bar",
      documentState: {
        // …
      },
    },
  ],
};
```

> Every cell in your diagrams is identifiable by two "coordinates", the step and the navigable (ID). I would have expected us to be able to reflect that in the spec, i.e. by having the traversible hold the equivalent of the diagram somehow...

I don't think you often want to pinpoint an item by two coordinates. These seem to be the common operations:

- The traversable is on step 3. Which history item should this navigable be displaying? This involves getting the history items associated with that navigable and getting the history entry that has the greatest step that is `<=` 3.
- Get the next/previous history step. This involves getting all the steps in order, and finding the step number that's next/previous. For app history you'd do this with the set associated with a single navigable.

> Here is the simplest way I see to translate your diagrams into "computer science concepts" while still letting their nature shine through:
>
> * Each traversible holds a map from navigable IDs (including the traversible itself) to lists of session history entries.
> * Each session history entry has a step.
> 
> Then you can do operations like:
> 
> * To get all session history entries for a given navigable, just look it up in the map.

It makes that particular operation simpler, but it makes other operations more complicated.

Let's say `location.replace('/foo')` is called. The history tree needs to be updated. In the model in this PR I would:

1. Let _entries_ be "get session history entries" for the navigable.
1. Let _newEntry_ be a new history entry, with new document state etc, and give it the same step as the current entry.
1. Replace the current entry in _entries_ with _newEntry_.

But with your flattened model:

1. Let _entries_ be the entries for this navigable (by looking it up in the map).
1. Let _docStateIsRedundant_ be true.
1. For each _entry_ of _entries_:
    1. If _entry_ is the current entry, continue.
    1. If _entry_'s document state is the current entry's document state, then set _docStateIsRedundant_ to false and break.
1. If _docStateIsRedundant_ is true, then:
    1. Let _redundantNavigables_ be the result of posting tasks into the document, and their descendant documents, to discover all the navigables that are about to be discarded.
    1. For each _redundantNavigable_ of _redundantNavigables_, remove _redundantNavigable_'s id from the traversable's map.
1. Let _newEntry_ be a new history entry, with new document state etc, and give it the same step as the current entry.
1. Replace the current entry in _entries_ with _newEntry_.

I tried to avoid stuff like above by structuring the model so things that were 'owned' by something were nested within it. That way, by removing one thing, all the things it 'owns' are removed too, and the bookkeeping doesn't need to be done manually like above.
